{"ts":"2026-08-31T10:42:15.276Z","author":"claude-orchestrator","author_source":"asserted","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_instance":"c41a528b9f424137989cc889","agent_provenance":{"model":null,"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"argv"},"topic":null},"op":"create","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/id","value":"pm-rl-bjl7"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/title","value":"pm-rl received no dependency updates at all, because it had no Dependabot configuration"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/description","value":"pm-rl had no .github/dependabot.yml at all, so it received no dependency updates of any kind: no npm bumps and no GitHub Actions bumps. Every sibling package in the fleet gets both. Two consequences. First, a vulnerable transitive dependency here is never reported and never bumped, and nothing distinguishes a repository with no findings from a repository nothing ever scanned. Second, when a codeql workflow is added here it would inherit the fleet-wide split-pair deadlock, because Dependabot opens github/codeql-action/init and github/codeql-action/analyze as two separate pull requests and the two must carry the same version, so each one alone fails the codeql check. Measured 2026-08-31 in unbraind/pm-gantt-chart 87 and 88. This adds npm and github-actions ecosystems on a weekly schedule, with a codeql-action group so that deadlock can never arrive here."},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/type","value":"Issue"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/status","value":"open"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/priority","value":2},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/tags","value":[]},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/created_at","value":"2026-08-31T10:42:15.276Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-31T10:42:15.276Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/author","value":"claude-orchestrator"}],"before_hash":"3cc22dff72be7b14824654a7a64ea62b04799939b2fee54c1b5f52ca60bf6df0","after_hash":"9853b24c300cb80d914571a7f363cf